    "en": {
      "category": "Health and prevention",
      "subcategory": "Infections & vaccines",
      "title": "That antibiotic for your cold might be borrowing against your future immunity",
      "excerpt": "Antibiotics don't work on colds — many of you probably already know that. But what if taking them anyway didn't just fail to help, but actually cost you something?",
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "A high fever and a throbbing throat that make it impossible to work or even get through the day — \"I just want relief as fast as possible, and ideally, the strongest medicine available.\" Throughout my career, I've seen so many patients come in feeling exactly this way, and when you're that miserable, wanting to reach for the strongest thing available makes complete sense."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "That said, the idea that \"most colds are caused by viruses, so antibiotics don't really help\" has made its way into TV programs and magazines often enough that many patients already know it. More than once, I've had someone say \"I know it probably won't do anything anyway\" before I've even finished explaining."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "By the end of this post, you'll understand a reason that goes beyond \"it just doesn't work.\" Here's the short answer: antibiotics don't just fail to help with a cold — taking them when you don't actually need them may quietly cost you something."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Here's what I'll cover:"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "- A quick refresher: symptom relief vs. root-cause treatment\n- What actually cures a cold isn't the medicine — it's your immune system\n- Here's the twist: antibiotics also take out your gut bacteria\n- If you can rest, that really is the fastest path"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### A quick refresher: symptom relief vs. root-cause treatment"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Medications broadly play two roles:"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "- **Root-cause treatment**: attacking the actual cause of an illness (e.g., antibiotics for a bacterial infection)\n- **Symptomatic treatment**: easing painful symptoms like fever, aches, or coughing (e.g., fever reducers and cough medicine for a cold)"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Over 90% of cold symptoms are caused by viruses. Antibiotics are a weapon built specifically for bacteria, so when the actual culprit is a virus, they simply miss the target as a root-cause treatment. Many of you may already know this part."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### What actually cures a cold isn't the medicine — it's your immune system"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "So how does a cold get better? The answer is your own immune system. Lowering a fever slightly to conserve your strength, calming a cough so you can sleep well at night — the real job of symptomatic treatment is to set the stage so your immune system can work at its best. The medication isn't curing you; your immune system is winning the fight against the virus, supported along the way by the medication."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### Here's the twist: antibiotics also take out your gut bacteria"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Research over roughly the past decade has revealed something worth knowing. Antibiotics don't just target the harmful bacteria they're aimed at — they also reduce the bacteria living in your gut that your body actually needs (your gut microbiome). And this gut microbiome isn't just about digestion; a growing body of research shows it plays a deep role in the development and regulation of your immune system."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "What's even more striking is that the effect doesn't simply reset within a few days of finishing the course. Studies have found that even a single, few-day course of antibiotics can leave the gut microbiome's composition altered for months — and in some cases, more than a year — before it returns to its original state."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "In other words: if you take an ineffective antibiotic for a cold \"just in case\" or because you want to get better faster, it does nothing for the cold in front of you — while potentially chipping away, little by little and without you noticing, at the very immune foundation you'll need to fight the next infection. If the medicine you took to recover quickly ends up nudging you, over time, toward a body that has a harder time recovering — that would be a shame, wouldn't it?"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### If you can rest, that really is the fastest path"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "If your symptoms aren't too severe and you're still eating and sleeping normally, there's no need to force yourself onto more medication. Giving your body real rest may look like the slow route, but it's actually the fastest one. I hope you'll see the choice not to prescribe an antibiotic as an active medical decision — one made to protect both your own future immune resilience and the broader fight against antibiotic resistance."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### In closing"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Let me add one thing. What looks like a cold can sometimes turn out to be something like strep throat, a bacterial illness that genuinely requires antibiotics. When I do prescribe one, I make a point of explaining why a bacterial infection is suspected in that particular case. If that explanation hasn't come up and you're curious, please feel free to ask, \"What is this medication actually treating?\""
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "To be honest, the actual decision is often a hard one. Some people might well have recovered without antibiotics, while among those who don't get better without them are cases where antibiotics truly work — tonsillitis, strep infection, sinusitis and pneumonia. That's why I listen carefully to how symptoms have progressed, and when antibiotics are needed, I use them without hesitation."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Wanting relief quickly when you're unwell is only natural. I'd like to stay close to that feeling while we think together about what treatment truly serves not just who you are today, but who you'll need your body to be tomorrow."
        }
      ]
    },
