    "en": {
      "category": "What I value in care",
      "title": "Why do the same words reach you differently in the exam room? — the other healing power of a “place”",
      "excerpt": "On the night after the dentist shows you how to brush, you somehow brush more carefully than usual. Ever had that happen?",
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "On the night after the dentist shows you how to brush, you somehow brush more carefully and for longer than usual. Yet a week later, you find you've slipped back into your old way of brushing. Ever had that happen?"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "You didn't forget what you were taught. What changed was the *place* you were in."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "This article explains why things you hear in the exam room make perfect sense, yet once you're home you drift back into old habits — and how to make the most of your visits. Here's the short answer: **the exam room is a special “place” where a switch for facing your own body turns on naturally. And borrowing the power of that place is, I believe, an important part of treatment.**"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### “Doctors are powerless outside the hospital”"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "“Doctors are powerless outside the hospital.” You sometimes hear this. It means that without testing equipment or medicines, there's only so much a doctor can do."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "But I feel there's another meaning in those words. Say a doctor tells relatives at a family gathering, “You might want to cut back on drinking a little.” Usually, it gets laughed off with “Yeah, yeah.” Even though what's being said is exactly the same as in the exam room."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "It's not that doctors are powerless because they can't treat anyone there. I think **they become powerless because there's no “place” for the words to land**."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### The exam room: a place a little apart from everyday life"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Daily life is full of work deadlines, housework, family plans, TV and smartphones, and tasty things in the fridge. You know your health matters, but it inevitably gets pushed back. That isn't weak willpower — it's simply human."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "But when you open the exam room door, the air changes a little."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "The squeeze of the blood pressure cuff on your arm. The test results laid out in front of you. A few minutes facing a doctor in a white coat across the desk. Work and housework don't come in here. **For as long as you're in that room, your own body sits at the center of the conversation.**"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "At that moment, for many people, a switch for facing themselves turns on naturally. There's quiet time to look back at the weight you'd been avoiding, or the evening drinks that had crept up."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### Why the same words sound different"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "When your family says, “Maybe go easy on the salt?”, you might brush it off with “I know.” But when you hear the same thing in the exam room, it somehow sinks right in. Have you felt that?"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Your family's words are by no means less important. If anything, they come from the people closest to you who worry most. What's different is how you're *positioned* to receive them."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "At home, you're relaxing as “a member of the family.” In the exam room, you're sitting there as “someone seriously thinking about their own health.” **The exam room is like a front-row seat for receiving health advice.** The same words land differently depending on which seat you hear them from."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### A clinic is a “place to reset”"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Seen this way, I feel a clinic's role isn't only to hand out medicine."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Once a month, you look again at the life that has drifted bit by bit, and set it right here. It's **a space for resetting your daily life**. I've written about why a monthly review supports treatment in “[The real treatment for lifestyle diseases happens outside the consultation room](/blog/hontou-no-chiryou).”"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "And this is also a place where it's okay to talk about what didn't go well. A single remark like “Honestly, last month was a total failure” often reveals the next step. I wrote about that in [“I've already done everything I can” — it's okay to say that in the exam room](/blog/honne-ga-ienai)."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### A word from the exam room"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "The switch that turns on in the exam room will slowly switch off again once you're home. That's perfectly fine. That's exactly why we open the exam room door again today, and treasure our time talking with you."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Please make use of this place as somewhere to switch your health back on. Kibana Nomura General Medicine Clinic hopes to be a clinic that values the power of such a “place.”"
        }
      ]
    },
